findings.claim:
The sheet shows O1's offset only as a 20 mm dimension on a flat silhouette, cut off 90 mm below the canopy and seen square to the cables. That is its worst direction, and the sheet doesn't say so. Three things are never shown:
- the cord then runs on a slant to the lamp's center, nearly parallel to the near cable;
- how big the offset looks from where people stand or sit;
- from below, the offset shows from every side.
'Option 1 keeps the canopy' also undersells a change the owner would see.

O2's extra 12 mm (1/2 in) reads as a slightly thicker canopy, not a fault.

How it looks:
- the visible band grows 24-25% (77 to 96 arcmin at 2 m under a 2.4 m ceiling; 61 to 76 arcmin from a stool under 2.7 m);
- the whole outline grows 8-13% standing and 3-6% from a stool;
- the proportion goes from 1:3 to 1:2.4;
- it adds 1.5% to the ceiling-to-rim height.

It also leaves the installer 13.5 mm over the splice instead of 1.5.

What it costs:
- about 90 g (3 oz) more on the ceiling;
- the site's Canopy line changes from 2.0 in deep (150 x 50) to 2.4 in (150 x 62);
- the Drop line changes from 30.6 in (776 mm) ceiling to rim to 31.0 in (788 mm), unless the standard cord is cut 12 mm shorter;
- the canopy weight line changes.

I found no well-made single round pendant whose cord leaves its canopy a little off center (UNVERIFIED negative). The normal single round canopy has a centered cord. Off-center cords show up only where the offset is the point or follows a cable:
- offset pendants meant to hang away from the ceiling box;
- cable kits with a power hole beside a center cable hole;
- linear fixtures whose cord is clipped to one cable.

